cartridge "MATCH MANIA/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1989"

' MATCH MANIA
' Based on "Match Mania", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, June 1989 (Commodore 64
' BASIC; orig/ has the program from the magazine's disk, detokenized).
' No author is named in the listing or on the disk menu. Made for the
' ColecoVision in Amy (the language by Amy Bienvenu) by Claude (an AI
' made by Anthropic) for Amy Studio, 2026.
'
' Kept from the listing: one or two players; the size of the patterns
' chosen first, characters per line and lines per box (lines 170-230);
' with two players, the winner by time or by % correct (lines 250-280);
' four framed boxes numbered 1-4, in two alternating frame colours
' (lines 400-680); each round takes a set of look-alike characters, in
' the order of DATA 1910 (QW, two T-joints, AS, OPL and a corner, two
' quarter blocks, IU, two checkers, a triangle and Z, two more joints,
' AE, V and a cross); the pattern is random characters of the set; one
' line R3 of one box R4 has one character changed: from a random start
' column, the first character equal to a random symbol R1 becomes
' another symbol R2, and if there is none the choice is made again
' (lines 770-990); the second player gets a new pattern from the same
' set (SKIP); the round's time counts from the moment the boxes are
' full; every guess counts, a wrong one says SORRY... TRY AGAIN; the
' right one shows CORRECT! LINE r CHAR c IS DIFFERENT, marks that
' character in all four boxes, the odd box flashes, then the guesses
' (1 GUESS... EXCELLENT!) and the time; 45 seconds are added to the
' time for each wrong guess (lines 1780-1790, marked with *); % correct
' = 100 - INT((guesses - rounds) / guesses * 100) (line 2080); the
' results table: player, rounds, guesses, % correct, time, winner or
' tie (lines 2070-2410).
'
' Liberties: joystick instead of number keys and names (PLAYER 1 and
' PLAYER 2); 1-12 characters per line (the listing 1-16: a box is 12
' tiles wide here); the 45-second penalty is always on (in the listing
' it was a choice for time games only; without it, guessing fast would
' win); a box guessed wrong is marked NO and can't be picked again (the
' listing counted the same wrong number again); one game is the eleven
' sets once, then the results (the listing looped the sets until "0"
' was typed); one player wins with 75% correct or better (the listing
' had no goal for one player; guessing averages 40%); the flash is
' 2.5 Hz (the listing's was too fast); characters are tiles drawn after
' the PETSCII shapes; a title tune ("Odd One Out", original).
'
' Controls: either joystick. Title: up/down choose a line, left/right
' change it, a button on START or CREDITS. Game: the stick moves the
' yellow frame to a box, a button guesses it.
'
' House rules kept: joystick only; text and tiles in columns 2-29 and
' rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the frame loop runs with the NMI off
' outside the wait; screens are drawn with the NMI off.

